| D-030 | 2026-09-29 | delivery-orchestrator (option chosen by the user) | **The whole agent process runs in a kernel-enforced process sandbox, so file-tool writes are confined to the role's scope whatever the write guard decided** (F-DG0-145, High). The guard checks a path and the CLI writes it afterwards; the agent's shell could swap a symlink in between. The user chose option 1 (2026-09-29): `run-agent.sh` now starts `claude` inside bubblewrap, built per run by the new `tools/agents/agent_sandbox.py`. The guard hook and the Claude Code Bash sandbox (D-025) remain as defence in depth.<br>• **Filesystem:** the root is read-only. `/tmp` and `/var/tmp` are private tmpfs mounts, so no other run's private TMPDIR is reachable; only the run's own is bound in. `HOME` is read-only except a private per-run session directory bound at `~/.claude/projects`, so `--resume` works and no other session's transcripts are reachable. Every repository root and worktree is read-only.<br>• **Confined roles** (reviewers, auditor, analyst): the working tree's top level is a throwaway tmpfs whose entries are the real ones bound read-only. The CLI's Bash sandbox needs to create mount placeholders there for missing dangerous files, and anything else written there vanishes. Only the role's areas are bound read-write: its evidence directory, plus QA's `tests/qa` and `e2e`, plus the analyst's `docs/analysis` and `handbacks`. Review records, the auditor's gate record and the analyst's register are written into a private staging directory bound over `docs/delivery/reviews/<stage>`, `docs/delivery/gates` or `docs/delivery`. A single-file bind is not possible, because the CLI's Edit writes a temporary file next to its target and renames it. After the run the runner copies back only new `round-N/<role>.*` files (write-once), the auditor's `<stage>.json`, and the analyst's `requirements.csv` if it did not change concurrently. Everything else is discarded and listed in `sandbox.json`, and a review or analyst run with discards exits 73 without auto-commit.<br>• **Implementers:** the working tree is read-write, with every existing path of the Bash deny list bound read-only on top.<br>• **Process:** only `CAP_SETFCAP` is kept, in the bounding set too (`--cap-drop ALL`, then `setpriv`). The Bash sandbox needs it to map uid 0 into its user namespace. `no_new_privs` is set. The `/sys/fs/cgroup/memory/process_api` cgroup is bound read-write because the CLI's Bash tool registers processes there. The network stays shared, because the CLI must reach the API; agent shells still have none (the Bash sandbox unshares the network and bridges the API over a unix socket).<br>• **Transparent to the PID/IPC namespaces, host procfs bound (nested bwrap).** The sandbox binds the host's real procfs (read-write; the CLI needs a working `/proc`) and creates **no** PID or IPC namespace. It is not a fresh `--proc` mount and carries no read-only submount over `/proc/sys`, because either makes the procfs "not fully visible" and the kernel then forbids the fresh procfs mount a reviewer's own bwrap needs: the pre-freeze (`tools/gates/sandbox-run.sh`) and the sandbox tests nest inside the Claude Code Bash sandbox and mount their own fresh procfs. Two earlier designs — a private procfs under an unshared PID namespace, then a fresh `--proc` with `/proc/sys` read-only — each failed inside a real reviewer's shell (`Can't mount proc … Operation not permitted`); this one was verified to work there. Sharing the host PID/IPC namespaces and exposing a read-write host `/proc` does not weaken write confinement (the read-only binds and the capability drop do that, independent of the PID namespace); both are disclosed in the threat model. **`/proc/sys` tunables** are read-only for the agent's **shell** (the Bash sandbox's user namespace, as in round 15) and inside the reviewer's own fresh-procfs sandboxes, where candidate code runs (`sandbox-run.sh` mounts a fresh procfs with `/proc/sys` read-only). A **file-tool** write to a uid-0-writable `/proc/sys` entry (directly or via a symlink race) is a disclosed residual — an availability / host-tunable risk, not repository or gate integrity — which round-16 code-security assesses. This is option A, chosen by the user on 2026-09-29 over keeping `/proc/sys` read-only in the process layer (which would break the reviewers' nested bwrap). Test spawns are time-bounded, so a sandbox that fails to build fails the test rather than hanging it.<br>
• **Inner Bash sandbox:** for confined roles, `agent_settings.py` omits missing deny paths below the top level. Their parent is read-only in the process sandbox, and bubblewrap could not create their placeholders there.<br>• **Evidence and validation:** `runs/<DGx>/<run-id>/sandbox.json` is hashed into `meta.process_sandbox_sha256`. The validator requires that the gate's review and audit records come from runs that were confined (read-only root, the host procfs bound (`procfs: host-bind`), no PID/IPC namespace of the sandbox's own, private sessions, only `CAP_SETFCAP`, no_new_privs), whose writable areas and staged areas are exactly the role's own, and that discarded nothing.<br>• **Environment:** this container was fresh, so `bubblewrap` 0.9.0 and `socat` were reinstalled; `setpriv` comes from util-linux. | **Verified in this container:**<br>• As root, a plain `bwrap` keeps every capability and `mount -o remount,rw /` succeeds inside it. With the capability drop above it is refused, including from a nested user and mount namespace.<br>• A real agent run through the runner, in a throwaway clone: Write and Edit worked in the evidence directory; its own review file was copied back, bound to its transcript (`tool_authored`) and auto-committed; Bash worked nested; `/var/tmp` showed only the run's own directory. An analyst run edited `requirements.csv` twice through staging.<br>• The agent's command runs in a further user and mount namespace created by the CLI's `apply-seccomp` helper, and reports a full `CapEff`. Those capabilities cover only its own namespace, whose mounts are locked copies: remounting read-write, unmounting and writing a read-only bind were all refused. The process-API cgroup is writable for the CLI process only, not from the agent's shell.<br>• **Race:** the round-15 reproduction was re-run with the real guard hook, 150 attempts per scenario. Without the process sandbox, 64 of 69 hook-allowed writes landed in `tools/gates/tests`. Inside it, across five scenarios (another run's TMPDIR, another reviewer's evidence, `tools/gates/tests` for QA and for an implementer, `.git/hooks`), the kernel refused 336 hook-allowed writes that the flipper had redirected, and none landed.<br>**Evidence:** `tools/agents/tests/process-sandbox.test.mjs` (the write-scope confinement, the symlink race with its positive control, the capability/namespace lockdown, the host-procfs/transparent-PID check, implementer scoping, the analyst register staging, the auditor gate record), the sandbox-run tests including "D-030: candidate code runs with /proc/sys read-only", the runner tests "F-DG0-145: the runner runs the whole agent inside the process sandbox and records it" and "D-030: a review run whose staging copy holds another role's file exits 73 and commits nothing", the validator test "F-DG0-145/D-030: gate records must come from runs whose whole agent process was confined to the role's own areas", and the settings and meta unit tests. The core F-DG0-145 tests fail on the previous code (`docs/delivery/test-evidence/DG0/orchestrator-probes/d030-tests-on-previous-code.txt`). The full reviewer stack (nested bwrap: pre-freeze and sandbox tests) is verified by the real-agent probe under `docs/delivery/test-evidence/DG0/orchestrator-probes`. | bwrap or setpriv becomes unavailable: the runner exits 65 before starting the agent. A CLI release that needs further writable paths fails closed (the agent cannot write), and is not worked around by widening the binds without a new decision. |
| D-031 | 2026-09-29 | delivery-orchestrator | **Round-16 repairs to the process sandbox: the copy-back is bound to the staging seed (concurrency), and the IPC namespace is unshared** (F-DG0-148 and the round-16 copy-back exit-73 false positive).<br>• **Copy-back concurrency (must-fix).** `agent_sandbox.py` `finish()` used to compare the staging copy against the **live** real directory. When reviewers run concurrently (a supported mode, §0.2 / D-028), a record another review commits to the real tree between this run's `prepare` and `finish` appeared as "removed" and the run exited 73 without auto-committing (observed in round 16: code-security's staging, seeded before domain committed, then saw domain-reviewer.json in the live tree). `finish()` now records a **seed** signature of every staged file at `prepare` and compares the staging copy against that seed, never the live tree: a file present in the seed but gone from staging is ignored (the real file is never deleted), and a file the run created or changed in staging is copied back only if it matches the role's accept pattern and is not a write-once collision. A file another run commits concurrently is never seen. Test: `tools/agents/tests/process-sandbox.test.mjs` "F-DG0-145 (round 16): a file another run commits to the real dir between prepare and finish is not a false discard" (fails on the previous code).<br>• **F-DG0-148 (Low): `--unshare-ipc`.** The process sandbox now creates its own IPC namespace. The IPC namespace does not affect procfs "full visibility", so this closes the SysV-IPC / POSIX-message-queue cross-run vector of threat-model residual 8 at no cost to the reviewers' nested bubblewrap. The summary records `unshare: ["ipc"]`, and the validator requires a gate record's run to include it.<br>• **F-DG0-147 and F-DG0-149 (Low, non-mandatory)** are left as accepted observations: F-DG0-147 (the copy-back accept pattern is not round-pinned) is fail-closed — the validator rejects a cross-round own-role file because it cannot reconstruct from that round's transcript; F-DG0-149 restates residual 7 exactly as disclosed. Both are re-verified/accepted in round 17. | Found in round 16 by code-security (F-DG0-147/148/149) and by the orchestrator (the copy-back exit-73 concurrency false positive). **Evidence:** the new process-sandbox test (fails on the previous code); the full suites (gates + agents + Python) pass; the round-17 pre-freeze and real-agent probe. | The IPC unshare breaks the CLI in some future environment: it fails closed (the run cannot start) and is revisited, never dropped silently. |
{+| D-038 | 2026-09-30 | delivery-orchestrator | **Round-21 repairs: the "history damage" was mostly a SHALLOW CLONE; the D-037 commit tolerances are tightly scoped and hardened (F-DG0-158 Med/mandatory, 160/161/245 Med, 159/162/163 Low).** Round 21 confirmed the D-037 fixes work for their intended cases (qa verified F-DG0-241/242/243/244 CLOSED_VERIFIED; code-security verified F-DG0-157), but found the tolerances were too broad and rested on a wrong premise.<br>• **The premise was wrong (F-DG0-160, Med).** This container's clone is **shallow** (`git rev-parse --is-shallow-repository` = true; `.git/shallow` = `e8361b8`, "DG0 round 12"). The pre-round-12 commits D-035/D-037 called "truncated/pruned" are simply **beyond the shallow boundary**, not gone: `git fetch --unshallow` restored the full 194-commit history and every one of them (`73c48546…`, `6c67698…`, `d1352f48…`, …) is a genuine **ancestor of HEAD**. So in a complete clone — which CI uses (`fetch-depth: 0`) — those ancestry checks pass on their own and need **no** tolerance. The commit-absence tolerances, and the write-once/back-dating checks, silently depended on clone depth. **Fix:** `validateGate` now **refuses a shallow repository** (`isShallow()`), so the gate is always judged against the complete history. The ONE genuine anomaly the full history leaves is the D-034 round-18 rewrite orphan `450c756` (round-18's `source_commit`), reachable only from the local `backup/pre-writeonce-repair-*` branch, which was **deleted** so `450c756` is uniformly absent in every clone of the branch (removing the clone-ref dependence of F-DG0-161).<br>• **F-DG0-245 (Med) + F-DG0-159 (Low): scope the commit-absence tolerance.** D-037's `commitPresent()` was a blanket skip for **any** unresolvable value in **any** round, so `checkInvocation` accepted a missing / `null` / `unknown` (run-agent.sh's rev-parse fallback) / `HEAD` / typo'd `head_commit_at_start`, and `checkClosure` a typo'd or all-zero `fix_revision`, **including in the gate round** — reopening the F-DG0-212 run-binding control. Now the tolerance applies **only** to (a) a well-formed `^[0-9a-f]{40}$` value — a missing or malformed one is a hard error — **and** (b) a round that is itself absent (its own `source_commit` does not resolve, threaded in as `binding.roundSourceCommit`), never a retained round and never the gate round (whose `source_commit` checkCandidate requires present, F-DG0-241). A present fix that is not in the candidate is still caught. Tests: validator.test.mjs "D-038 / F-DG0-245" and "D-038 / F-DG0-159".<br>• **F-DG0-158 (Med, mandatory): no silent finding deletion.** D-037's `recordless` exemption let a findings sidecar with no `<role>.json` record excuse a "dropped" finding — but it could not tell "never imported" from "imported, then deleted", so **any** finding could be removed from `findings.json` with no trace. The exemption is **withdrawn**: every finding a sidecar raises must be in `findings.json`. The one genuinely-never-imported interrupted-run finding, **F-DG0-238** (a duplicate of CLOSED_VERIFIED F-DG0-150), is instead **imported** into `findings.json` and closed by its reporter (qa) in round 22, not exempted. `collectVerifications` still skips a record-less **verifications** sidecar (import-findings never attributes one).<br>• **F-DG0-161 (Med): F-DG0-150/151 made clone-independent.** They are re-verified by code-security in round 22 against the current candidate (fix `421c922`, an ancestor of HEAD), so their closure no longer depends on the orphaned round-18 commit; plus the backup-branch deletion above removes the last conditional presence of `450c756`.<br>• **F-DG0-162 (Low): residual 7 corrected.** It is now **verified** (not just theorized) that the agent's **Bash shell**, as well as a file tool, writes uid-0 DAC-writable host-global `/proc/sys` entries **directly** (a plain redirect, no race); the round-15 "read-only for the shell" claim is **wrong and withdrawn**. The `agent_sandbox.py` comment now attributes the read-only `/proc/sys` a reviewer's nested `sandbox-run.sh` sees to its **explicit `--ro-bind /proc/sys`**, not a userns default.<br>• **F-DG0-163 (Low): schema subset validator.** `additionalProperties:false` used `props[key]`, so an extra key named after an `Object.prototype` member (`constructor`, `toString`, `__proto__`, `hasOwnProperty`) resolved to a truthy prototype value and was silently accepted; it now uses `Object.hasOwn(props, key)` (and `Object.hasOwn(value, key)` for `required`). Test: validator.test.mjs "D-038 / F-DG0-163". | Found in round 21 by code-security (F-DG0-158..163) and qa (F-DG0-245); domain PASS. All were fail-open on a mechanically-enforced control or clone-dependent, none wrongly approved a gate. **Evidence:** the unshallow (`git rev-list --count HEAD` 114→194; the named commits now ancestors); the scoped validator + its tests; the real-repo dry-run on the full history (0 pruned/record-less/dropped besides the round-18 orphan, which the backup-branch deletion + round-22 re-verification clear); the round-22 pre-freeze. | The tolerance now covers only genuinely-superseded rounds beyond the shallow boundary or orphaned by D-034; a complete clone is required for the gate, so ordinary validation never relies on it. If a future rewrite orphans a round again, its findings are re-verified in a retained round rather than tolerated. |+}
| D-037 | 2026-09-30 | delivery-orchestrator | **Round-20 repairs: the DG0 gate could not pass on this branch's real history; the pruned-commit tolerance is completed and the delivery metadata corrected (qa's dry-run findings F-DG0-242/243 High, 244 Medium, 241 Low; F-DG0-157 Low).**<br>• **What qa found.** Round-20 qa drafted a schema-valid APPROVED gate on the frozen candidate and ran `validateGate` on a fresh clone (the auditor's real check) — which `validate.mjs --stage DG0` had never reached because DG0 had no gate record. It reported ~263 errors, most of them permanent gate blockers from the pre-existing history damage that D-035 only partly addressed.<br>• **F-DG0-242 (High): complete the pruned-commit tolerance.** D-035 tolerated a genuinely-missing commit only in `findManifest`; `checkClosure` (a CLOSED_VERIFIED finding's `fix_revision` must be an ancestor of the round/gate `source_commit`) and `checkInvocation` (a run's `head_commit_at_start` must contain the manifest) still hard-failed on pruned commits — 132 + 63 errors, because 66 closures' `fix_revision`s and 63 runs' `head_commit_at_start`s were truncated (pre-round-12) or rewritten (D-034). Both now tolerate a **genuinely absent** commit (new `commitPresent()` helper, `git cat-file -e <sha>^{commit}`) while still running the check whenever the commit **is** present, so a reachable fix that is not in the candidate, or a run that started before the manifest, is still caught. Same principle and safety as D-035. Test: validator.test.mjs "D-037 / F-DG0-242 …" (present-but-not-in-candidate → still caught; pruned → tolerated; short/non-hex → still rejected).<br>  Two closures the dry-run then surfaced were **present-but-off-branch**, not absent, so the tolerance rightly did **not** hide them: F-DG0-150/151's `fix_revision` still named `cb76260…`, the **pre**-D-034-rewrite round-17 repair commit (kept alive only by the `backup/pre-writeonce-repair-*` branch), which is not an ancestor of HEAD. The D-034 rewrite re-committed that same repair as `421c922…` (byte-identical `agent_sandbox.py`, an ancestor of HEAD), so per this finding's option (b) their `fix_revision` was **re-pointed to the retained commit** `421c922…` — a metadata correction (identical fix content), leaving them CLOSED_VERIFIED.<br>• **F-DG0-243 (High): the D-034-kept record-less sidecars no longer block.** The round-17/18 `qa-verifier.{findings,verifications}.json` (kept by D-034 because deleting committed review files violates write-once) each tripped "record … not listed in review_rounds", and F-DG0-238 (raised in the round-17 qa findings sidecar, never imported) was reported "dropped". `collectVerifications` and `collectRaisedFindings` now **skip a sidecar whose own `<role>.json` record file does not exist on disk** — the signature of an interrupted run whose verdict record was never written — while a sidecar whose record file exists but is unlisted is still an error. import-findings already never imported those record-less **verifications**, so nothing in `findings.json` depends on them. **Findings** sidecars are handled to match import-findings exactly (which imports a findings sidecar's findings with or without a record): `collectRaisedFindings` still processes a record-less findings sidecar (so a finding that WAS imported and later closed, e.g. F-DG0-239 raised in the interrupted round-18 qa run and verified in round 19, is not falsely reported "no reviewer sidecar raised it"), but a finding raised **only** by a record-less sidecar and absent from `findings.json` is not a "dropped" finding (`recordless` flag → checkFindings skips the drop error) — it was simply never imported, exactly as import-findings left it. So F-DG0-238 (record-less round-17 sidecar, a duplicate of CLOSED_VERIFIED F-DG0-150, never imported) raises no error, and every finding that IS in `findings.json` still requires a raising sidecar. Test: validator.test.mjs "D-037 / F-DG0-243 …" (record-less verifications and findings sidecars tolerated; once a `<role>.json` record exists the inconsistency is caught again); the real-repo dry-run confirms F-DG0-238/239 clear.<br>• **F-DG0-244 (Medium): delivery-metadata gaps corrected (no candidate change to these).** The round-19 reviewers' shell test evidence was committed (it had been left untracked, so the verification evidence of F-DG0-152/236/237/239 was absent from clones). Every CLOSED_VERIFIED finding now carries a **full** 40-hex `fix_revision`: F-DG0-152/153 → `1a99d13e…`, F-DG0-239 → `38ffd1da…`, F-DG0-154/155/156/240 → `4008588a…`, and F-DG0-232 (previously null) → `f764edf…` (its AN-11 register fix, a reachable ancestor).<br>• **F-DG0-241 (Low): gate `source_commit` must be a branch-reachable commit object.** The D-036 check used `git cat-file -e`, which also resolves an off-branch commit or an annotated tag that peels to the frozen commit (QA20-S7, QA20-S8). It now requires the object type to be exactly `commit` (`git cat-file -t`, new `objectType()` helper) **and** an ancestor of HEAD (`isAncestor`) — note `merge-base --is-ancestor` peels a tag, so the type check is what rejects the annotated-tag id, and the ancestry check is what rejects the off-branch commit (which passes locally but is absent from every fresh clone). A missing commit fails the type check. Tests: validator.test.mjs "D-037 / F-DG0-241 …" (off-branch commit and annotated tag) plus the retained D-036 missing-commit test.<br>• **F-DG0-157 (Low, accepted in round 20): leftover wording fixed.** The `agent_sandbox.py` comment and threat-model residual 7's D-033 credit were corrected to the host-global framing (a host-global `/proc/sys` write has no cross-run *amplification* distinct from the own-process write). | Found in round 20 by qa (real-repo dry-run gate, `docs/delivery/test-evidence/DG0/qa/tests/real-repo-gate-blockers.mjs`). All errors were fail-closed (nothing wrongly approved). **Evidence:** the dry-run categorisation; the new validator behaviour; the round-21 pre-freeze and a clean dry-run gate. | If a later check needs a pruned commit's tree, it cannot be recovered; the tolerances keep content-integrity checks (manifest entries ↔ candidate_id, review binding, findings resolution) and only relax the provenance re-checks that require the vanished commit. |
| D-036 | 2026-09-30 | delivery-orchestrator | **Round-19 repairs to the D-033/D-034/D-035 changes the reviewers found (F-DG0-152 verified; F-DG0-240/154 Medium, 155/156 Low).**<br>• **F-DG0-240 (Medium, qa) + F-DG0-155 (Low, code-security): the gate's `source_commit` must exist, in every mode.** D-035 made `findManifest` tolerate a missing `source_commit`, and `findManifest` runs for **every** recorded round including the gate round; meanwhile `checkCandidate` in **current** mode recomputes the candidate from the working tree, not from `gate.source_commit`. Together these let current-mode validation (the mode the auditor runs before approval) accept an APPROVED gate whose `source_commit` names a non-existent commit — a real regression from D-035 (before it, `findManifest` rejected this). **Fix:** `checkCandidate` now requires `gate.source_commit` to resolve to a real commit (`git cat-file -e <sha>^{{commit}}`) in **both** modes; the D-035 tolerance is thereby non-gate in effect. The gate's own candidate is still recomputed from the working tree (current) or from the retained `gate.source_commit` (historical). Test: `tools/gates/tests/validator.test.mjs` "D-036: a gate whose source_commit does not exist is rejected in current mode too".<br>• **F-DG0-154 (Medium, code-security) + F-DG0-149: threat-model residual 7 scope corrected.** The D-033 edit wrongly narrowed the `/proc/sys` write residual to "its own run". `/proc/sys` is **host-global kernel state**: a uid-0 DAC-writable tunable a file tool could reach affects the whole host, not just the writing run — so the residual is larger than the earlier wording, and the unverified round-15 claim that `/proc/sys` is read-only for the agent's **shell** is **withdrawn**. Residual 7 now states the accurate host-global availability / kernel-tunable scope (still not repository, reviewer-artefact or gate-record integrity, all of which are read-only binds / private tmpfs independent of `/proc/sys`); F-DG0-149 is this residual at its corrected scope, for the round-20 reviewers and auditor to accept as an observation or reject. Most `/proc/sys` tunables need `CAP_SYS_ADMIN` (dropped) and are not writable; the residual is the subset writable by uid 0 via DAC. It is inherent to option A (a read-only `/proc/sys` submount breaks the reviewers' nested bwrap).<br>• **F-DG0-156 (Low, code-security): the git allow-rules were removed from `.claude/settings.json`.** The `Bash(git filter-branch|branch|push|update-ref:*)` rules the user added for the one-time D-034 history rewrite reached every project agent (loaded from project settings), though the agent sandbox has no network and cannot reach a remote. They are no longer needed (round 20 is an ordinary repair round: commit + fast-forward push, no rewrite), so `.claude/settings.json` is back to `Bash(bwrap:*)` only. | Found in round 19 by qa (F-DG0-240) and code-security (F-DG0-154/155/156); F-DG0-152 (High) was CLOSED_VERIFIED and the D-034 history repair confirmed sound in the same round. **Evidence:** the new validator test; the corrected threat-model residual 7; `.claude/settings.json`; full suites and the round-20 pre-freeze. | If a reviewer judges the corrected (host-global) residual 7 unacceptable under option A, that reopens the option-A trade itself and is escalated to the user, not worked around. |
| D-035 | 2026-09-30 | delivery-orchestrator | **The validator tolerates a review round whose `source_commit` was pruned from history, keeping every other manifest check.**<br>• **What was found.** `checkReviewRounds` → `findManifest` recomputes each recorded round's manifest from its `source_commit`. This branch's history was **truncated before round 12** in an earlier session (the earliest retained commit is "DG0 round 12 …"; 91 commits total), so the `source_commit`s of rounds **1–12** no longer exist as objects; round **18**'s commit was rewritten by the D-034 write-once repair. In a fresh clone (CI, a reviewer's clone, the gate audit) the recompute therefore failed for all of these, which would have **blocked the DG0 gate at the first completed audit** — a latent, pre-existing condition, not caused by any single change, surfaced now because DG0 has never reached a gate.<br>• **Why tolerating is safe.** The recompute-from-`source_commit` is a *redundant* cross-check. Every load-bearing property is kept: the manifest's `entries` must still self-consistently hash to its `candidate_id` (`findManifest`), the manifest's `candidate_id`/`frozen_at`/`source_commit` must still equal the review-round record (`checkReviewRounds`), reviews remain write-once and bound to the run that reviewed the candidate, and — crucially — the **gate candidate is recomputed independently from the retained `gate.source_commit`** in `checkCandidate`, which is untouched. A superseded review round approved nothing, so being unable to re-fetch its (pruned) commit does not weaken any approval.<br>• **The change.** In `findManifest` (`tools/gates/lib/rules.mjs`), when the `source_commit` recompute throws, tolerate it **only if the commit is genuinely absent** (`git cat-file -e <sha>^{commit}` fails); every other recompute failure — including a *reachable* commit whose content does not match (still line 807) — remains a hard error. Test: `tools/gates/tests/validator.test.mjs` "D-035: findManifest tolerates a pruned (missing) source_commit but still enforces entries self-consistency" (present → clean; missing → tolerated; tampered entries → still caught). Round 18's `records` were also corrected to list only the two records that exist (domain, code-security), since qa produced no verdict record before the session limit.<br>• **For reviewers.** This is a deliberate, disclosed loosening of a redundant check on **non-gate** rounds only; if you judge it to weaken gate integrity, raise it — the gate candidate's own recompute is unaffected. | Found by the orchestrator while validating a fresh clone before the round-19 freeze (rounds 1–12 + 18 "cannot recompute from its source_commit"). **Evidence:** `git rev-list --count HEAD` (91) and `git log --reverse` (earliest = round 12); a fresh-clone `checkReviewRounds` before/after the change; the new validator test. | If a future check needs a pruned commit's tree, it cannot be recovered; the manifest's `entries` remain the record of what that round reviewed. |
